der to undermine  russian information operations from your  perspective  what are the steps that need to be taken  in order  to counter this and and what can be done  to protect the free flow of news and  information  i think one of the biggest tools that  the kind of liberal democratic world has  to counter this different disinformation  specifically on social media is the fact  that social media companies are based in  liberal democracies um they are  accepting of course for the child so uh  chinese social media companies they're  mostly based out of silicon valley um or  somewhere else in the united states and  that means that they must respond to  regulatory pressure they must respond to  policy makers they must respond to  legislation  um  the greatest tool that we have in order  to really ensure that the social media  companies clean up their act that they  stop giving a platform um to obvious  conspiracy mongers um obvious peddlers  of disinformation is to mandate them in  some way and i'm not a policy expert um  don't take that my word is gospel but  there must be a real conversation about  a legislative approach or regulatory  framework that can be applied to these  social media platforms uh that will  somehow  constrain the operation especially of  bots because a lot of this  discrimination is not being shared  organically it is first boosted to an  insane degree um by bot networks and  then once that initial engagement has  been registered by the algorithms only  then is does it begin to get traction  organically and spread from there so  there  has to be something to constrain the  activity of these bots often these are  networks um as we know in russia these  are networks funded and operated by  state actors uh and taking anything less  than a regulatory or legislative  approach to that problem is not going to  fix it you can't rely on the uh tech  companies themselves the social media  platforms themselves to self-regulate  because  first their incentives are not there to  do so and secondly  they are not state actors and they don't  really have the resources necessary to  counter a state working on a  disinformation  direction on their own  rebecca we were speaking before about  the 2022 edition of the reporters  without borders world press freedom  index and one of the issues that that  highlights is what it calls the  disastrous effect of news and  information chaos so i want to ask you  what is the impact of a globalized and  unregulated online information space  that encourages fake news and propaganda  and how bad is it all getting  well that again is one of the major  themes that we highlighted in the index  is the impact of all of this on the  state of press freedom around the world  so when we talk about polarization we  mean exactly that the sort of um the  impact of  disinformation proliferating on social  media but also outright propaganda and  that's not just a matter of um internet  trolling that can be actually done on  official media channels and so  um this year we have seen that within  countries including our own democracy so  i know we're we're mostly talking about  russia at the moment but it's worth  noting that part of the trend is even  within our democracies the proliferation  of opinion media the sort of fox  nusification of media is also  contributing to this chaos and then of  course the propaganda wars of  authoritarian states including russia  against democracies it's all creating a  lot of tension when it comes to to  global press freedom so it's worth  noting that of course while the ukraine  uh conflict was preceded by an  information war this is not a new  phenomenon this has been building for  years when it comes to russia and it's  not only russia we've pointed for a  number of years to concerns with china  for example um reporters thought borders  published a landmark report just in  december uh really examining um  the the spread of china's model of  information control which china is now  trying to proliferate throughout the  region and in fact internationally as  well it's it's not just a russia problem  it's broader than we think um at  reporters without borders we do think  that um there needs to be greater  responsibility of the platforms what  that looks like um it has to be done in  a way that protects press freedom and  freedom of information at the core we've  engaged in advocacy around the european  dsa for example which we think currently  looks like a good model of how this  could be accomplished here in london i'm  watching carefully what the uk  government is doing with the the online  safety bill we've got a few concerns i  have to say  but it's clear that this space does need  some regulation  so tim you heard rebecca there talk  about the fact that these online spaces  uh from her vantage point uh need more  regulation i also heard romeo talk about  that there should be frameworks put in  place to help safeguard information  that's out there i want to ask you you  know there's growing calls for countries  to adopt legal frameworks and systems to  protect information online to to counter  disinformation of course one example  rebecca mentioned the dsa uh um  with the eu but but do you think that  we're going to see more of this is this  trend going to continue are there  countries out there that are going to be  setting up systems or passing  legislation in order to do this  well it's absolutely necessary for these  kinds of regulatory frameworks to be  introduced and i just want to give a  quick word about what they need to look  like as well as the fact that they need  to exist because up until now we've both  relied upon platforms to moderate  themselves and also taken a really  piecemeal approach to content moderation  which looks at specific pieces of  content or types of content and tries to  remove them rather than examining the  underlying business models of the  companies rather than looking at how  their own models and how their own  algorithms can encourage the kinds of  problems that we're seeing so what we  really need is a systemic approach which  allows us for example as researchers to  access data to encourage rigorous levels  of transparency so that we can see just  how big the problems are and how they  are functioning and because all this  data shouldn't be in-house at ten  companies it should be available for  scrutiny um by those who are qualified  to look at it and so we need to make  sure that these regulatory frameworks  enshrine that um in law and i really do  hope that in addition to the dsa in  addition to the online safety bill that  other countries with clout and with  large numbers of users in them start to  adopt these because the power that they  have is directly proportional to how  much of the market of a tech company um  is regulated so the uh the dsa in europe  means much more for example than the  omni safety bill in the uk if the us  weren't to introduce similar kinds of  legislation that would have much more  power still partly because of these  country companies being based primarily  in silicon valley and then i guess if i  may a word about uh tiktok which is a  bit of a problem because of the fact  that it is chinese owned  it doesn't have the same kinds of  regulation or accessibility as nearly  any other platform it's the most opaque  you basically have to do any kind of  research on it manually we have no  conception of the scale of the problem  on there but the manual qualitative  research that we have done shows that  there are huge amounts of propaganda  being pumped onto the platform um from  state actors and non-state actors like  marguerite to simonian um the the editor  of russia today or rt um has an  extremely popular account on there even  with rt and sputnik being banned  it's important to note that this  platform in particular has become a  massive new vector for disinformation  and we really need to do something about  that romeo it looked to me like you were  reacting to some of what tim was saying  did you want to jump in  no i just wanted to to mention that yes  um  as tim said i think  taking this uh individualized approach  to um creating regulatory frameworks for  these uh social media platforms i mean  twitter and facebook don't work alike  tick tock and instagram don't work like  and there is no  one size fits all framework you can  place on all these platforms and say  well the problem solved you do have to  approach them  individually based on how the platform  operates and i think this is an  incredibly  important insight to remember when we  consider kind of what the the future of  digital communication is going to look  like and now that  we have  uh matured enough to to encounter these  massive massive information problems  rebecca so in april the european  commission announced that it wants to  see the eu adopt a law to protect  journalists and civil rights activists  from lawsuits aimed at censoring them  but in order to take effect  this proposal is going to need approval  from eu member countries and from  lawmakers in the european parliament  first and foremost do you think that  this is going to happen and secondly how  significant would it be if it does  you'd have to scrutinize the the bill in  question but in principle of course  there is a need for greater protections  um in particular uh for journalists  online  and  i i don't know if that particular bill  is focused just on the sort of  disinformation side if it possibly might  extend to for example um uh covering  slap strategic lawsuits against public  participation because i know there's  been a lot of advocacy uh towards um  securing protections against slaps uh  particularly that that target  journalists at the eu level um from from  my perspective that would be a positive  thing but again we have to see what is  tabled um and and to assess indeed what  support it has and if that might then be  possible not only at the european level  but possibly replicable elsewhere  rebecca i also want to ask you um from  your vantage point how much better  equipped are our fact-checking  organizations now at dealing with  disinformation whether it's surrounding  the war in ukraine whether it's  surrounding the pandemic other  propaganda efforts just how much better  have fact checkers online gotten at  countering uh propaganda and  disinformation  well from reporters without border side  we haven't actually focused on  fact-checking initiatives because our  press freedom mandate means that we  focus on journalism and frankly if  something has not been fact-checked it  is not professional journalism um we  have worked instead to develop the  journalism trust initiative which is  essentially  a white listing exercise we've developed  a standard initially at the european  level but which we're now working to  roll out more globally that looks to  sort of encourage and incentivize  incentivize um journalism that has gone  through a rigorous chat to show that it  is about professional ethical standards  of journalism so we feel strongly that  it shouldn't be about the content but  about the process because then you  address the content issues that arise  but if you only address content as  fact-checking initiatives do that is  only addressing the symptom and not the  disease  tim the u.n has said that online attacks  on journalists and information are  eroding trust from your vantage point  how bad are things getting  i think that's extremely important to  note because fact-checking is one thing  and so is stopping frivolous or overly  litigious lawsuits but we have to  address the root cause of this which is  at least to some degree that trust in  journalism trust in democratic  institutions trust in electoral  processes  is really being eroded on a systematic  level by a variety of actors  and the demand side of disinformation  isn't being addressed either so it's not  just that people are being supplied  a range of different narratives from a  range of different um bad actors it's  also that  they are looking for that kind of  information because in many ways a lot  of things in the world feel like they're  getting worse people feel like they  can't trust authority authorities  and so consequently they end up going  looking for things which make totalizing  uh conclusions about the world which see  in black and white terms which often  appeal to conspiracy theories um because  those give them more satisfying  explanations than many of the things uh  many of the real mechanisms which  underpin the way the world works and so  that's deeply problematic and and we  know that the many of regimes and also  many governments are in and and  political parties in states are engaging  in these sorts of campaigns to undermine  trust we saw it during the trump years  in the us we see it with russia right  now we see it with for example north  african regimes like the eritrean regime  we see it in eastern europe it with  hungary or central rio rather  and that's deeply problematic so i think  we are in a really quite dire state  right now with respect to the amount of  trust that we place in journalists and  the amount of attacks that happen um in  the online space as well as offline as  well as the sort of prosecutions and  other kinds of lawsuits  romeo do you think we're at a moment  where we'll see a drive by more members  of the international community to  actually strengthen protections for  journalists and for journalism  unfortunately um i can't  really say that i'm optimistic  uh in this sense  there has been a consistent trend over  the past few years  in basically the opposite direction  there has been um a huge movement which  uh  is based around the  kind of concept of free speech or at  least that's the rhetoric that they use  and you saw some of this with um when  billionaire elon musk uh said he was  going to buy twitter and bring free  speech back  but that rhetoric actually serves to  obscure  uh the actual  practical effects of what these  so-called free speech policies would  bring which would be an end to  moderation or incredibly hands-off  moderation which in turn will only uh  intensify attacks and densify dog piling  intensify smears and further erode trust  in any kind of institution including  journalistic ones so  unless governments really  take a  long-term and  uh  kind of layered or nuanced look  situation instead of simply bowing to  the rhetoric oh we need more free speech  we need less regulation uh they're  controlling your speech unless that kind  of rhetoric is ignored and um the  question is probed further by  policymakers  then i don't see that situation  improving especially when it has  um such you know wide support from uh  very influential individuals in the  public sphere rebecca how much uh have  surveillance and hacking compromised uh  journalists of late and and not just  journalists but also  their sources and and whistleblowers how  much dangerous has how much more  dangerous has it gotten for them all  it is certainly increased um i think  tensions it sometimes can lead to  self-censorship if if somebody feels  very um  ill at ease in investigating for example  certain risky topics or individuals um  it's also worth mentioning the impact  immediately that all of this has on the  safety of journalists um in parallel to  trolling this act of sort of abuse and  threats and these you know vast pylons  that can happen very easily towards  journalists and in particular um often  disproportionately targeting women  journalists uh too often now can  translate into real-life manifestation  of such threats and sometimes violent  acts and so in our broader work in the  sort of more classic areas of press  freedom such as safety of journalists is  also having a massive impact as well um  it's all part of the evolving nature of  threats to journalism and certainly  states are not uh doing enough at least  in a concrete way beyond just statements  we need to see more action including by  our democracies uh to ensure that um  their their own house is in order with  the domestic press freedom climate and  that includes um ensuring that the  online space uh is a safe and enabling  environment for journalists to be able  to do their jobs  all right well we have run out of time  so we're going to have to leave the  conversation there today thanks so much  to all of our guests rebecca vincent  romeo kokriatsky and tim squirrel  and thank you two for watching you can  see the program again anytime by  visiting our website aljazeera.com and  for further discussion go to our  facebook page that's facebook.com  forward slash aj inside story you can  also join the conversation on twitter  our handle is at aj inside story  how much i'm doing the whole team here  in doha bye for now  [Music]  you
 

